{
 "id": "2026-10-25-why-sky-blue",
 "topic": "why the sky is blue (Rayleigh scattering, 1/λ⁴)",
 "subject": "physics",
 "hook_type": "everyday mystery",
 "caption": "Air molecules scatter light in proportion to 1/λ⁴, so blue light (450 nm) scatters about 5.9 times more than red (700 nm). That scattered blue reaches you from every direction, and at sunset the long path through air strips it away.\n\nSend this to the friend who asked why the sky is blue.\n\n#physics #science #optics #rayleighscattering #light #stem #sky #learnphysics",
 "cover_at": 1.0,
 "hook": "Why is the sky blue and not white?",
 "sub": "The answer is a fourth power.",
 "beats": [
  {
   "text": "Sunlight is a mix of every color, and air molecules are over a thousand times smaller than its wavelength.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "sz",
     "tex": [
